### What is batch processing?

Batch processing takes a bounded dataset and runs a job over all of it. It optimizes for throughput: it can crunch enormous volumes because it sees the whole dataset. The latency is high because results are available only after the job completes.

```java
package com.bank.processing;

// Spark-style: process a full day's transactions at once
public class BatchInterestCalculation {
    public void run() {
        Dataset<Row> txns = spark.read().parquet("/data/transactions/2026-10-31");
        Dataset<Row> interest = txns.groupBy("accountId")
            .agg(sum("amount").multiply(lit(0.0001)).as("dailyInterest"));
        interest.write().parquet("/data/interest/2026-10-31");
    }
}
```

### What is stream processing?

Stream processing consumes an unbounded flow of events and computes results incrementally as each event arrives. It maintains state over time, uses windows to group events, and must handle late data.

```java
package com.bank.processing;

// Kafka Streams-style: react to each transaction within seconds
public class StreamFraudDetection {
    public void build(StreamsBuilder builder) {
        builder.stream("transactions")
            .filter((key, txn) -> isSuspicious(txn))  // react immediately
            .to("fraud.alerts");                       // emit alert
    }
}
```

### When should you use batch?

Use batch when you need to analyze large historical datasets, when results can wait minutes or hours, and when correctness over the complete dataset matters: end-of-day interest, regulatory reports, reconciliation.

### When should you use streaming?

Use streaming when you need to react within seconds: fraud detection, real-time dashboards, live alerts, and event-driven triggers.

### How do they compare?

| Aspect | Batch | Stream |
|---|---|---|
| Data | Bounded (fixed dataset) | Unbounded (continuous flow) |
| Latency | High (job must complete) | Low (seconds) |
| Throughput | Very high | High |
| Correctness | Exact (sees all data) | Handles late data with watermarks |
| Engine | Spark | Flink, Kafka Streams |
| Banking use | Interest, reports | Fraud detection, alerts |

### What is windowing in stream processing?

Windowing groups events by time intervals. A tumbling window is non-overlapping fixed intervals. A hopping window overlaps. A sliding window moves continuously.

